### 🗑️ **CA REMOVAL (NEW SUBCOMMAND)**
- [ ] Implement `remove` subcommand to delete a CA and unregister it.
  - [ ] CLI: `simpleca remove <id> [--force] [--dry-run] [--unregister-only]`
    - [ ] Positional `<id>` required (aligns with `use <id>` style).
    - [ ] `--force`: skip interactive confirmation (required in non-interactive mode).
    - [ ] `--dry-run`: print what would be deleted/unregistered without changing state.
    - [ ] `--unregister-only`: remove from registry but keep files on disk (administrative cleanup).
  - [ ] Behavior:
    - [ ] Validate that the CA exists (registry or on-disk dir `${CA_DATA_ROOT}/cas/<id>`).
    - [ ] If CA is current default, either:
      - [ ] refuse removal unless `--force` and unset default; or
      - [ ] require setting a different default first. Decide policy and implement accordingly.
    - [ ] When deleting files, remove `${CA_DATA_ROOT}/cas/<id>` recursively; leave other CAs untouched.
    - [ ] Update registry: `del(.cas[id])`; if `defaultCa == id`, set `defaultCa = null`.
    - [ ] Without `jq`: best-effort fallback behavior:
      - [ ] If registry missing, or no jq, and `--unregister-only` used: emit actionable error that jq is required for unregister; otherwise proceed with on-disk deletion only.
  - [ ] Safety/UX:
    - [ ] Print a summary of what will be removed; request y/N confirmation unless `--force`.
    - [ ] Make removal idempotent (no error if directory already gone and registry lacks entry when `--force`).
    - [ ] Exit non-zero on partial failure; suggest manual cleanup when applicable.
  - [ ] Tests:
    - [ ] Unit: parsing; missing id error; refuses when default and no `--force`; `--unregister-only` without jq path; `--dry-run` no changes.
    - [ ] Integration: create CA, `remove <id>`, verify `list` no longer shows it and directory is gone; remove default CA and ensure default clears.
  - [ ] Docs/Help:
    - [ ] Document irreversible action; recommend backups via `export` before removal.
    - [ ] Add examples to main help and README.

